    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="25" relname="preparation">Great article thanks.</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="27" relname="span">I found this article in Scientific America web page.</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="2" relname="background">It is interesting that the IEEE to fix the election quagmire we find ourselves in.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="27" relname="elaboration">http://www.sciam.com/print_version.cfm?articleID=0001CD43-EAA5-1006-A8B383414B7FFE87</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="6" relname="preparation">Below is an excerpt from the article:</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="28" relname="span">To develop standards that all voting machines would meet, the Help America Vote Act turned to the Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers (IEEE). Project 1583 is the resulting effort and is intended, the IEEE summary says, to assure confidentiality, security, reliability, accuracy, usability and accessibility. To set standards, an IEEE working group first puts together a draft proposal, which it sends out for public comment. Then the draft must pass a vote by the members of the standards association, a subset of the IEEEs worldwide membership.</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="39" relname="span">There are multitudes of references on the web that refer to flaws in the electronic voting system.</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="7" relname="elaboration">The machines in Florida, as well many other states such as Illinois, are by Election Systems &amp; Software of Omaha, Nebraska.</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="39" relname="elaboration">John Hopkins had been involved in studies indicating there were flaws with the software in the machines as well.</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="11" relname="concession">In fairness to the company there was an upgrade applied in order to correct the problem</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="40" relname="span">but I never really saw articles indicating that the fix was a good one.</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="31" relname="condition">If one steps back and looks at the situation</segment>
        <segment id="13" parent="31" relname="span">perhaps we can say that we rushed to electronic voting too quickly.</segment>
        <segment id="14" parent="32" relname="span">We had a very bad experience in Florida that was live on national and international view for weeks.</segment>
        <segment id="15" parent="34" relname="volitional-cause">We wanted to have it correct it yesterday.</segment>
        <segment id="16" parent="35" relname="span">Hence, there was not enough time to train the poll workers who in general are senior citizens.</segment>
        <segment id="17" parent="36" relname="contrast">We emphasize that it takes time to implement change management in an organization</segment>
        <segment id="18" parent="36" relname="contrast">and we pretend to do this across and entire state and the nation.</segment>
        <segment id="19" parent="37" relname="span">The concepts of HCI to involve users in the development plan did not seem very visible here.</segment>
        <segment id="20" parent="19" relname="concession">At the sake of talking out both sides of my mouth I must say that the electronic machines have not produced any hanging chads.</segment>
        <segment id="21" parent="42" relname="span">I hope we can come up with a better system for the 2008 elections.</segment>
        <segment id="22" parent="21" relname="elaboration">Maybe there is hope with IEEE involved.</segment>
        <group id="23" type="span"/>
        <group id="24" type="span" parent="23" relname="span"/>
        <group id="25" type="span" parent="43" relname="span"/>
        <group id="26" type="span" parent="25" relname="span"/>
